A mall food court and New Orleans Cajun-fusion favorite perfected for the pressure cooker. Juicy chicken thighs soak in a decadent glaze made from Kentucky bourbon, brown sugar, soy sauce, garlic, and cider vinegar. Cooking under pressure keeps the chicken succulent, while a quick final sauté reduces the sauce into a thick, restaurant-quality glaze.

*Adapted from [Delish](https://www.delish.com/cooking/recipe-ideas/a41576330/instant-pot-copycat-bourbon-chicken-recipe/).*

## Ingredients

### Bourbon Sauce Slurry
- ½ cup low-sodium chicken broth
- 2 tablespoons cornstarch
- ½ cup packed light brown sugar
- ¼ cup tomato ketchup
- ¼ cup low-sodium soy sauce
- 1 teaspoon ground ginger
- ¼ teaspoon kosher salt
- ¼ teaspoon freshly cracked black pepper

### Chicken & Sauté Base
- 1 tablespoon vegetable oil
- 4 cloves garlic, finely minced
- ⅓ cup bourbon whiskey (such as Jim Beam, Maker's Mark, or Bulleit)
- 2 tablespoons apple cider vinegar
- 3 lbs boneless, skinless chicken thighs, trimmed of excess fat and cut into 1-inch chunks

### To Serve
- Cooked Jasmine rice or mashed potatoes
- 3 scallions (green onions), thinly sliced

## Instructions

1. **Whisk the Glaze Mixture:** In a medium bowl or 2-cup liquid measuring cup, whisk the chicken broth and cornstarch together until the starch is completely dissolved with no sediment. Add the light brown sugar, ketchup, soy sauce, ground ginger, kosher salt, and black pepper. Whisk vigorously until the sugar crystals blend into the sauce.
2. **Sauté Garlic and Deglaze with Bourbon:** Turn your Instant Pot on and select the **Sauté** function (Normal setting). Add 1 tablespoon of vegetable oil. Once shimmering, add the minced garlic and sauté, stirring constantly with a wooden spatula for about 1 to 2 minutes until fragrant and pale golden (do not let it burn). Pour in the ⅓ cup bourbon and 2 tablespoons apple cider vinegar. Let the alcohol boil rapidly for 2 full minutes to evaporate the harsh alcohol fumes and reduce the liquid by half, using your spoon to scrape up any browned garlic from the bottom of the pot.
3. **Add Chicken and Sauce:** Press **Cancel**. Drop the cubed chicken thigh pieces into the pot. Pour the whisked sauce slurry directly over the chicken and give it a thorough stir so all pieces are coated.
4. **Pressure Cook on High:** Lock the Instant Pot lid in place and ensure the steam release valve points to **Sealing**. Select **Manual / Pressure Cook** on **HIGH** pressure and set the time to **4 minutes**.
5. **Timed Natural Release:** When the 4-minute timer beeps, allow the Instant Pot to naturally release pressure for 10 minutes. Afterwards, carefully turn the steam valve to **Venting** to vent any remaining steam. When the pin drops, unlock and remove the lid.
6. **Sauté to Thicken the Glaze:** Press **Sauté** again. Bring the liquid in the pot to an active rolling simmer. Cook uncovered for 8 to 10 minutes, stirring frequently to prevent the bottom sugars from burning. As the excess moisture boils away, the sauce will deepen in color and reduce into a thick, lustrous, sticky lacquer that coats the back of a spoon and blankets every bite of chicken. Press Cancel.
7. **Plate and Garnish:** Portion warm steamed rice or buttered mashed potatoes into bowls. Ladle generous mounds of the chicken and rich bourbon glaze over top. Scatter with sliced fresh scallions and serve hot.

## Notes

- **Chicken Thighs vs. Breasts:** Boneless chicken thighs are strongly recommended because they remain juicy and tender under pressure without drying out during the reduction step. If using chicken breasts, reduce the pressure cook time to 3 minutes.
- **Non-Alcoholic Substitute:** You can substitute apple juice or additional chicken broth mixed with 1 tablespoon of apple cider vinegar for the bourbon, though bourbon lends authentic caramel depth.
